course: dictionary
lesson: 12978
title: Exonerated: shown not guilty
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: When someone is blamed for something they did not do, they can be exonerated. It means they are officially shown or declared not guilty. «After three years in prison, Sam was exonerated when DNA evidence proved he had never been at the scene.» The clues are prison, DNA evidence and never been there: Sam was innocent all along, and the court said so.
explain: Exonerated can also mean freed from blame in everyday life. «The pupil who broke the window owned up, so Ella was exonerated.» Ella had been blamed, but the real culprit confessed, so she was no longer to blame. Try swapping in cleared of blame: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: You can say exonerated of something. «She was exonerated of the theft when the real thief was found.» Notice the word of: it tells you what the person was accused of. Words like fully exonerated mean completely cleared, with no doubt left at all.
fact: Exonerated means officially shown or declared not guilty, or freed from blame.
fact: Exonerate is the verb, and exonerated is its past form: «The judge exonerated the man yesterday.»
fact: You can be «exonerated of» a crime or a wrong, such as «exonerated of cheating».
fact: «Exonerated by DNA evidence» means proved innocent by scientific tests.
fact: The opposite idea is being found guilty or being blamed for something you did not do.
exercise: «The jury heard the new evidence, and Mark was exonerated.» What does exonerated mean? | Given a prize for winning | *Officially shown not guilty | Sent to prison for a long time | Found with the stolen bike
exercise: «Tom was accused of lying. In the end he was exonerated.» What happened in the end? | Tom was blamed again | *Tom was shown not to be guilty | Tom was sent to the head teacher
exercise: «The court exonerated the innocent man after the new evidence.» Find and copy the word that shows what the court did. | court | innocent | man | *exonerated
test: «After the video was checked, the security guard was fully exonerated.» What does fully exonerated suggest? | Partly blamed still | Sacked from the job | *Completely cleared of blame | Asked to watch the video again
test: Ella says exonerated is just another word for forgiven. Why is she probably wrong? | Exonerated means you are sent to prison | *Forgiven means you did something wrong, but exonerated means you were not guilty | Forgiven and exonerated mean exactly the same | Exonerated is a kind of sport
exam: «The prisoner was exonerated of the crime after DNA evidence showed he had been far away.» Which word tells you the prisoner was NOT the criminal? | far | prisoner | *exonerated | crime
